  "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{18}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(1+6n)\\pi }{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\u2026,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{18}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{18}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{18}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ (written in ascending order) are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{18},\\frac{7\\pi }{18},\\frac{13\\pi }{18}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{18}+\\frac{13\\pi }{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\pi }{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
